# `show` reads the log as it was and calls `save`, which writes on it and, while there is more to show, calls `show`
# again: read, then written, both. `save` is defined before `show` here and after it in `recursion-order-b`; the plan
# is the same. What the inner `show` reads is what `save` left, a value inside `save` (rule 4). The inner `show` is a
# closed step, its body drawn in the frame it stands in: it writes the log through the `save` it calls, reached.

route recura.run
step run/show
step run/show/save
choice run/show/save/?1
step run/show/save/?1/show recursive
line entry > run call
line File(data/log.txt) > run/show own
line run/show > Screen own
line run/show/save > File(data/log.txt) own
line run/show/save > File(data/log.txt) in run/show/save value
line File(data/log.txt) in run/show/save > run/show/save/?1/show value
line run/show/save/?1/show > Screen own
line run/show/save/?1/show > File(data/log.txt) reached
home File(data/log.txt) in run/show/save run/show/save

side entry left
side File(data/log.txt) both
side Screen right
